{{Politics of Okaiken}}
The '''Emperor of Okaiken''' is "the symbol of the state and of the unity of the people" according to the 1968 [[Constitution of Okaiken]], which established the current democratic framework following the dissolution of the military government. He is a ceremonial [[wikipedia:figurehead|figurehead]] under a form of [[wikipedia:constitutional monarchy|constitutional monarchy]] and is head of the [[Imperial House of Okaiken|Sanese Imperial Family]] with functions as [[wikipedia:head of state|head of state]]. He is also the highest authority of the [[wikipedia:Shinto|Sanese Animistic]] religion as he and his family are said to be direct descendants of [[Amaterasu]], the goddess of the sun and the universe.

The Emperor is called the '''''Tennō''''' (天皇) in Sanese, literally meaning "heavenly sovereign". He is also referred to in English as the '''Mikado''' (帝) of [[Okaiken]]. Currently, the Emperor of Okaiken is the only remaining monarch in the world reigning under the title of [[emperor]].

The Imperial House of Okaiken is the oldest continuing [[hereditary monarchy]] in the world. In [[Sanshoki]], a book of Sanese history finished in the eighth century, it is said that the Empire of Okaiken was founded in 695 BC by [[Toyama of Miyagi|Emperor Toyama]]. The current emperor is His Imperial Majesty the [[Emperor Jutsu of Mitoyagi|Emperor Masahito]], who ascended to the [[Golden Leaf Throne]] following the abdication of his father, [[Go-Shōkō of Mitoyagi|Emperor Go-Shōkō]], in 2019, marking the beginning of the [[Jutsu (era)|Jutsu era]].

The role of the Emperor of Okaiken has historically alternated between a largely ceremonial symbolic role and that of an actual imperial ruler. Since the establishment of the first [[shogunate]] in the medieval period, the emperors of Okaiken have rarely taken on a role as supreme battlefield commander. Sanese emperors have nearly always been controlled by external political forces. Specifically, from 1610 to 1870, the [[Honda Shoguns]] ruled as the military governors of the realm, creating a system of "Dual Sovereignty" where the Shogun ruled from [[Okami]] while the Emperor presided over spiritual matters in [[Toyozawa]] or [[Mito]].

Since the [[Saito Restoration]] in 1870, the Imperial Palace has been located in the heart of [[Okami]], built upon the site of the former Shogunal fortress.

[[The Emperor's Birthday]] is a national holiday.

==Role==
Unlike most constitutional monarchies, the Emperor is not even the ''nominal'' chief executive explicitly in the [[Constitution of Okaiken]]. The constitution states that the Emperor "shall perform only such acts in matters of state as are provided for in the Constitution and he shall not have powers related to government." Article 4 also states that these duties can be delegated by the Emperor as provided for by law. Article 65 explicitly vests the executive power in the [[Cabinet of Okaiken|Cabinet]], of which the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Minister]] is the leader.

In addition, while formally the Emperor's duties include appointing the Prime Minister to office, Article 6 of the constitution requires him to appoint the candidate "as designated by the Diet" (in practice, the candidate designated by the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]]), without any right to decline appointment. This is in marked contrast to his status under the [[Saito Constitution]] of 1889, which recognized the emperor as the sacred embodiment of all sovereign power.

The other detailed regulation of the Emperor's duties is laid down in Article 7 of the constitution, where it is stated that the "Emperor with the advice and approval of the Cabinet, shall perform the following acts in matters of state on behalf of the people:
#Promulgation of amendments of the constitution, laws, cabinet orders and treaties.
#Convocation of the Diet.
#Dissolution of the House of Commoners.
#Proclamation of general election of members of the Diet.
#Attestation of the appointment and dismissal of Ministers of State and other officials as provided for by law, and of full powers and credentials of Ambassadors and Ministers.
#Attestation of general and special amnesty, commutation of punishment, reprieve, and restoration of rights.
#Awarding of honors (such as the Order of the Paulownia).
#Attestation of instruments of ratification and other diplomatic documents as provided for by law.
#Receiving foreign ambassadors and ministers.
#Performance of ceremonial functions."

The Emperor is not the (ceremonial) commander-in-chief of the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]]. The Okaiken Self-Defense Forces Act of 1959 explicitly vests this role as commander-in-chief with the Prime Minister.

Regular ceremonies of the Emperor with a constitutional basis are the Prime Minister investitures in the Imperial palace throne room and the [[Speech from the Throne]] ceremony in the [[House of Officers (Okaiken)|House of Officers]] in the [[National Diet Building]]. The latter ceremony opens ordinary and extra sessions of the Diet.

==History==
===Miyagi and Ancient Period===
During the [[Miyagi Empire]] period (695 BC – 794 AD), the Emperor held absolute religious and tribal authority, unifying the clans of the archipelago under the worship of the Sun Goddess. The emperors of this era, such as the legendary [[Jingū of Miyagi|Empress Jingū]], were often warrior-monarchs who led expeditions into neighboring territories.

===The Dual Sovereignty (Honda Period)===
Following the turbulent [[Sengoku period]], the nature of the throne changed drastically. In 1610, the warlord [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]] ascended the throne, but shortly after enacted the "Edict of the Two Realms." This split the Imperial family into two: the Imperial Line (descended from [[Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi|Emperor Daigo]]) which held spiritual authority, and the Shogunal Line (descended from [[Honda Shigetomo]]) which held military power. For 260 years, the Emperor lived in semi-seclusion, legitimizing the rule of the Honda Shoguns.

===Modern Era===
The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1870 saw the resignation of the last Shogun, [[Honda Yoshinobu]], and the restoration of political power to [[Saito of Mitoyagi|Emperor Saito]]. This marked the beginning of the modern imperial state. However, following the defeat in the [[Great Adonian War]] and the rise of the "Taisei Yokusankai" military junta, the Emperor's role was redefined. 

The post-war constitution stripped the Emperor of political power, transforming the role into a purely symbolic one. The current Emperor, Masahito (Emperor Jutsu), is known for his scientific research and his modern approach to the monarchy, often breaking with tradition to interact directly with citizens affected by natural disasters.

